  "endpoint_note": "alpha=1.0 must equal eval.json's bm25 AUC and alpha=0.0 its semantic AUC. Asserted, not eyeballed: a blend reading the wrong scores would still produce a smooth, plausible curve.",
  "mechanism_note": "bm25_score_spread explains the split. BM25's value tracks how sharply it separates candidates WITHIN an impression, and the two languages fail in opposite ways: English is silent much of the time but decisive when it speaks; Danish almost always speaks and barely discriminates. A Danish score of 'some overlap with everything' is as uninformative as no score, but unlike a zero it does not fall through to the popularity tie-break.",
  "question": "does blending the lexical score into the semantic one beat semantic alone?",
  "report": "hybrid_ablation",
  "selection_note": "The test-split sweep picks its best alpha by looking at the number it then reports, so part of any gain there is having peeked. `val_selected` repeats the choice on the validation split -- untouched since Q1 for exactly this purpose -- and applies it to test. A gain that survives that is evidence; one that does not was an artefact of selection."
}
